<p>Mandan's first public school classes were held in the city's first City Hall, located in the 200 block of Ashurst Avenue, today's First Avenue NW. Some local historians, however, claim that instruction began in the city's first courthouse in spring 1877. Total enrollment was 12. Over the next few years, classes moved to a log cabin owned by {% person "j-j-mitchell" %}J. J. Mitchell{% endperson %} in 1879, the Methodist church basement in 1880, and the Presbyterian church basement in 1881. The Mandan School District, then and now known as Morton County District No. 1, was formally organized on April 23, 1881, during a meeting of town leaders at the Methodist church. By 1882, enrollment had grown to 60 students.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/300-first-school-1883-birdseye-view.jpg" alt="Mandan's first schoolhouse in an 1883 bird's-eye view">
    <figcaption>Excerpt from the 1883 Stoner bird's-eye view of Mandan</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The first schoolhouse was constructed in fall 1881 at the southeast corner of Wright Avenue, today's First Avenue NE, and Second Street NE. Completed in 1882, it cost more than {% inflation 4000, 1882 %}. It was described as built "in the approved modern style, is of two stories with a belfry and bell, and has large and commodious hall and stairway. The interior is supplied with the best style of desks and blackboards." The school was initially divided into two grades, and both were full by 1883.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/334-expanded-first-school.jpg" alt="Mandan's expanded first school building">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The first school board directors were {% person "daniel-flynn" %}Daniel Flynn{% endperson %}; treasurer {% person "henry-waldo-coe" %}Dr. Henry W. Coe{% endperson %}; and {% person "frank-j-mead" %}Frank J. Mead{% endperson %}. They approved a 26-by-30-foot addition costing $2,000, plus $1,600 for furniture. By 1884, the school served 60 pupils and offered a six-year course of study. In 1887, as the city's population approached 2,500, the school board began renting the Vinton Building in the Third Ward for an additional school. {% person "alexander-strahan" %}Professor Alexander Strahan{% endperson %} was principal of both schools in 1888.</p>

<p>A second schoolhouse opened on the west side of town in 1890 near Mitchell Avenue, today's Eighth Avenue NW, and Main Street. Known as the West End School, it was a two-room, two-story building converted from a house. {% person "margaret-bowers-bingenheimer" %}Miss Margaret Bowers{% endperson %}, later Mrs. George Bingenheimer, was the primary teacher and principal. She taught first and second grades to approximately 25 pupils in the ground-floor room and earned $55 per month. {% person "ida-ellison" %}Miss Ida Ellison{% endperson %} taught third, fourth, and fifth grades. All sixth graders attended the two-story Wright Street school alongside east-side first- through fifth-graders. The 1882 building, near St. Joseph's Catholic Church, thereafter became known as the East End School.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/708-1904-mandan-school-with-kids.jpg" alt="Students gathered outside Central School">
    <figcaption>Central School student photograph</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 1899, the Mandan Board of Education, presided over by {% person "c-e-v-charles-draper" %}C. E. Draper{% endperson %}, included {% person "theodore-a-cummins" %}T. A. Cummins{% endperson %}, {% person "j-h-mcgillic" %}J. H. McGillic{% endperson %}, {% person "h-h-harmon" %}H. H. Harmon{% endperson %}, and {% person "h-d-stevenson" %}H. D. Stevenson{% endperson %}. The board asked voters to approve construction of a {% inflation 15000, 1899 %}, three-story brick {% place "central-school" %}Central School{% endplace %} to house junior and senior high school students and provide a larger grade school for the west side.</p>

<p>In April 1909, {% person "william-huff" %}William Huff{% endperson %} moved the East End School to "Dogtown," a common name for the southeastern neighborhoods. The building was placed on the curve leading to the Heart River bridge to serve the city south of the railroad yard. The school board decided to construct a replacement, and classes temporarily returned to City Hall. In February 1910, the board instructed local architect {% person "c-a-bloom" %}C. A. Bloom{% endperson %} to prepare plans for a new two-story brick schoolhouse with a basement, four recitation rooms, and an office. Early estimates, including plumbing and heating, placed the cost above {% inflation 15000, 1910 %}.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/491-1918-custer-school-mandan.jpg" alt="Custer School in Mandan in 1918">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In April 1912, the city celebrated the opening of a new First Ward school named for General {% person "george-armstrong-custer" %}George Custer{% endperson %}. The dedication included a speech by {% person "james-martin-hanley-sr" %}Judge James Hanley Sr.{% endperson %} summarizing Custer's life and a short address in German by State's Attorney {% person "william-wild-bill-langer" %}William Langer{% endperson %}, intended for parents.</p>

<p>The district needed more space as both the city and the number of years students spent in school grew. Central School's downtown location prompted additional construction at the site. A new high school was added at the west edge of the lot in 1911, followed by a gymnasium in 1919.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/334-1912-high-school.jpg" alt="The 1911 high school addition beside Central School around 1912">
    <figcaption>1911 high school addition, c. 1912; Central School is at left</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/334-mandan-high-school-1934.jpg" alt="The new Mandan High School with Central School behind it in 1934">
    <figcaption>New high school in 1934; Central School is in the background</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/708-gpa-photo-2016.jpg" alt="Front entrance of the Great Plains Academy building in 2016">
    <figcaption>Front entrance of the Great Plains Academy building in 2016</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In 1934, high school classes moved to a larger Tudor-Gothic building designed by {% person "gilbert-horton" %}Gilbert Horton{% endperson %}. Horton, originally from St. Paul, Minnesota, relocated to Jamestown in 1911 and eventually designed 254 school buildings in North Dakota. The new school was constructed on the same lot in front of Central School. The former high school building on the east side became the junior high school, and Central School was used entirely for elementary grades. The complex later became {% place "great-plains-academy" %}Great Plains Academy{% endplace %}.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/708-stark-1951-usgs-topo.jpg" alt="1951 topographic map showing the Mary Stark School area">
  </figure>
</div>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/334-stark-syndicate-school-1922.jpg" alt="Syndicate School in 1922">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Syndicate School was constructed in 1922 in southwest Mandan, an area developed under the direction of the Boston Syndicate, a group of East Coast investors that included {% person "henry-endicott" %}Henry Endicott{% endperson %}. The brick building's three levels—two stories and a half basement—followed a common layout of the period. A classroom wing was added in 1964. In 1960, the school was renamed Mary Stark Elementary School after the death of {% person "mary-stark" %}Mary Stark{% endperson %}, a longtime, beloved teacher. The original structure was razed and replaced with a modern facility in 1970. The replacement was dedicated in January 1971 and remains substantially unchanged today.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/mandan-school-system-history/409-red-trail-elementary-apr2019-3.jpg" alt="Red Trail Elementary School in April 2019">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Parents and children in northwest Mandan and the surrounding rural area celebrated the opening of Red Trail Elementary School in August 2014. Designed with two sections for each grade from kindergarten through fifth, the school was full on its first day. During the 2018 school year, district enrollment increased by 200 students, with most of the growth occurring in north Mandan.</p>

<p>Construction began in July 2019 on an eight-classroom, $2 million addition. The project would add one section for each grade, while two extra classrooms would allow two grades to be divided into additional sections as demographics required. Completion was scheduled for August 2020.</p>
